    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\nRetaliation Threatened if the UK Joins EU Tariffs on Chinese Electric Vehicles\n28 photos\nPhoto: Lepas\nAsia's most economically powerful nation shipped 255,000 vehicles to the United Kingdom in the first half of 2026. That's 87 percent year over year, and just around 181,000 of those vehicles were optioned with electrified powertrains. Just like Europe, the showrooms in Britain are becoming an increasingly useful outlet for Chinese newcomers.\nLondon, however, finds itself between a rock and a hard place. Beijing has recently issued a warning, with the Ministry of Commerce threatening countermeasures if the United Kingdom imposes EU-style tariffs on Chinese-built electric vehicles. The ministry told Britain to avoid simply following the EU's lead, think twice, and follow the World Trade Organization rules.\nThe trigger? That came from UK trade minister Anas Sarwar, who said that Britain and the European Union should act in lockstep to address the People's Republic of China's growing influence. Sarwar also made it clear that London was discussing potential tariffs on Chinese electric vehicles with European partners.\nA policy change hasn't been announced, which is why China's response could very well be considered an overreaction. After all, Chinese automakers rely on exports as domestic electric vehicle demand continues to cool down amid intense price competition and fading subsidies. Furthermore, extended-range electric vehicles are getting increasingly popular with Chinese motorists.\nFor the time being, as highlighted by Automotive News, the United Kingdom still applies its 10 percent import tariff to China-made vehicles of all types. The EU has added specific duties aimed at Chinese battery-electric vehicles, and you are correct to view those duties as a protectionist response.\nPhoto: Lepas The dispute is tied to a larger negotiation over industrial policy, with Britain eager to join the EU's proposed Industrial Accelerator Act. Proposed by the European Commission in March 2026, this act's main targets are boosting domestic manufacturing while enhancing supply chain resilience. Simply put, said act favors Euro-made products for very sensible reasons.\nA Franco-German proposal would enable Britain's participation, but with a catch. That catch is to align with EU trade defense measures. The legislation remains under negotiation as of this writing. The alignment condition would pull British trade policy much closer to Brussels than London might otherwise choose.\nAs a case in point, the EU imposed additional countervailing duties of up to 35.3 percent on Chinese battery-electric vehicles. Add the standard 10 percent levy to that, and you get a maximum combined rate of 45.3 percent. If Britain were to follow suit, Chinese automakers would be greatly hindered by said tariffs.\nSpeaking of alignment, the European Commission plans an annual cap of 350,000 Chinese hybrid vehicles from December 2026, reducing China's share of the EU hybrid passenger vehicle market from 6 to 3 percent. Britain's participation would also hurt China, hence the ministry's rather aggressive reaction.\nAs it stands, China wants the UK to understand that trade restrictions come with consequences outside the automotive industry. While it has not named potential targets, China doesn't appear to be bluffing here. As for the UK, post-Brexit woes could very well be the catalyst for playing ball with the EU.\nVia // Automotive News\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nChina UK Europe industry business\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Pristine 1967 Dodge Coronet R/T Has a Rare Feature in Plain Sight",
